Z||000000||Uaana||11-11-2000||03:07 AM||||Need some help! Anyone here done a tuneup on a late model Dodge Stratus? Took it into the shop to have a check engine light checked and they said it would need a tune up bare min. problem is, The quotes I recieved for the tune up have ranged from 4-600+ dollers.<BR>It appears that you have to pull the intake manifold off just to change the plugs..<BR>And I thought the back three on the GT were a pain...<BR>Anyways any thoughts/ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||maninblack||11-11-2000||03:47 AM||||what year and milage is your car?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Uaana||11-11-2000||03:57 AM||||Sorry<BR>It's a 96 with about 85,000 on it.<BR>I asked if it was one of those auto on check engine lights. but all three shops I talked to said that those were only on 80's early 90's cars because they had trouble passing emissions and it was a way to make sure people got them tuned up. Newer cars only flash check engine if something is wrong||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||11-11-2000||08:25 AM||||Wow!  Why do you have to pull the manifold (intake or exhaust) to change plugs?  Have you read the codes?  Reading the fault codes on my '91 Dodge minivan (3.3L engine) was easier than on the Fiero. Maybe yours works the same.  With engine off, turn the key to ON-OFF-ON-OFF-ON in succession, then read your codes from the flashing check engine (or whatever they call it) light.  Codes should be availabe in Haynes manual or maybe on line somewhere.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||11-11-2000||10:08 AM||||Not sure which engine you have in the Stratus but I have a 95 Intrepid with the 3.5DOHC. To change the plugs you need to remove the spark plug wire at the coil pack then pull from between the intake runner to allow you to pull up the spark plug wire boot. It's not too bad to do. I also changed the wires when I tuned it up last time. Now that is a pain. Took forever to snake the wires thru the intake. I cannot beleive they remove the intake every time they tune-up a car. Everyone I know who has tried to take their car to a dealer for a tune-up has always told me they were going to charge $300-$400. It didn't matter what type of car it was or the size of the engine. The fact that most cars just need plugs, air filter, Fuel filter, oil, oil filter, and occasionally wires, seems that the money is way out. I did a major tune-up on my Intrepid, my wife's Durango, and my fiero for less than $300 in parts and it probably took less than 3 hours to do all! If I charged $300 per tune-up I could work 3 hours and almost afford a PlayStation 2 on Ebay!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
