Z||000006||Uaana||11-11-2000||12:14 PM||||well after searching a couple of "cloud car" msg boards "?? why would you want a weapon R air cleaner on a 4dr coupe" anyways i digress<BR>looks like the only way to change the plugs is to pull off the intake manifold end of story.. looks like a three hour job bare min.<BR>Why does this stuff always happen when it gets cold??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||11-11-2000||02:43 PM||||Pull an <B>intake manifold</B> to change the plugs?!  I kind of liked the looks of some the new Dodges, but now you've given me the Stratus fear  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>If that's true, well, so much for "forward thinking..."||||reg||11
Z||000008||Uaana||11-11-2000||04:26 PM||||Just in case anyone will ever need this info.<BR>"God have mercy on their souls"<BR>Tools needed:<BR>1/4 inch metric socket and drive set<BR>3/8 inch metric socket and drive set<BR>a pair of pliers<BR>5/8 inch spark plug socket<BR>(4 inch length) 1/4 in extension<BR>(6 inch length) 3/8 in extension<BR>3/8 inch universal joint (u-joint)<P>Note: Although the manufacturer recommends that the intake manifold gasket and the EGR gasket be replaced while performing this procedure, it is permissible to re-use these gaskets, provided the gaskets weren't damaged to begin with.<P>And now, the procedure to change out the spark plugs:<P>Remove the intake air duct from the throttle body<BR>Remove two wire connectors from the throttle body<BR>Remove two wire connectors from the right-hand side of the manifold<BR>Rotate the throttle to full-throttle position, then remove the throttle and cruise control cables<BR>Remove the emissions line from the top of the throttle body<BR>Remove the bolts holding the throttle cable bracket to the manifold<BR>Remove the bolts holding the EGR tube to the manifold<BR>Carefully pry the EGR tube from the manifold. If you wreck the gasket here, you'll need to buy a new one. Use a piece of masking tape the secure the EGR gasket.<BR>Remove 7 bolts from the top of the manifold<BR>Using the 3/8 inch extension and the u-joint (and a lot of swearing), remove two bolts from the rear of the manifold. You'll have to feel around for these bolts to get an idea where they're located<BR>Carefully rotate the manifold up, and disconnect the large vacuum hose in the rear, using pliers to release the clamp<BR>Remove any remaining emissions lines, and carefully remove the manifold. Place the manifold so that the gasket sealing surface is facing up, and cover to keep out foreign matter.<BR>Remove the distributor cap, and inspect the inside. If the cap inside contains any evidence of abnormal arcing, or is dirty, replace the cap and rotor (See below)<BR>One at a time, replace each spark plug and wire, following the directions given above<BR>Take the manifold, and carefully place it on the engine. Rotate, and install the large vacuum hose and any miscellaneous emissions lines.<BR>Carefully line up the manifold gasket and the intake manifold, then loosely install the manifold bolts. Remember that the longer bolts go into the higher holes, and the shorter long bolts go into the lower holes.<BR>Loosely install the two short bolts holding the manifold to the rear mount. Cursing has been found to be helpful here.<BR>Re-install all of the wire harness connectors and emissions lines<BR>Tighten the manifold bolts in three stages, working outward from the center, to a final torque of 160 in-lbf.<BR>Tighten the two short bolts to 160 in-lbf<BR>Remove the masking tape from the EGR gasket, then re-install the tube to the manifold. Torque the EGR tube bolts to 95 in-lbf<BR>Install the throttle cable bracket. Tighten the bracket bolts to 95 in-lbf<BR>Rotate the throttle to full-throttle position, then re-install the cruise control and throttle cable (in that order)<P>Re-install the intake air duct<BR>To change out the distributor cap, remove the spark plug wires, one at a time, from the old distributor, and install the wires in the same spot on the new distributor.||||reg||8
Z||000009||Standard||11-12-2000||03:42 AM||||better not hear ya *****  about changing the plugs in your GT after this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  If you need any help I'd be more than willing.  Just gimme an e-mail<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
