Z||000011||StuGood||11-12-2000||12:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Uaana:<BR><B>.. After this I'll never bich about the back three on the GT again</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, since you made this comparison, I guess it's okay to post this subject in a "Fiero-related" forum... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BTW, I seldom have any trouble with the back three.  The front three are usually harder  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  J/K- knew what you meant, of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <P>||||reg||12
Z||000012||Mark||11-12-2000||05:55 PM||||I remember the days of the V8 Chevy Monza....you had to unbolt a motor mount and lift the engine a bit to get at one of the plugs. That doesn't seem like such a big deal anymore!<P>One last suggestion.  What about going up from the bottom?  It sounds strange, but that was by far the easiest way to change the back three plugs on the Dodge Caravan (3.3L). There was just enough room to sneak your arms up between the engine and firewall.||||reg||1
